     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-18 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Haryana Government Employees Can Claim Medical Reimbursement for Parents Despite ₹3,000 Pension, High Court Rules</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Government Employees</span> <span>#Medical Reimbursement</span> <span>#Haryana High Court</span></div>
       <p>The Punjab and Haryana High Court has issued a significant ruling affirming that Haryana government employees cannot be denied medical reimbursement for their dependent elderly parents solely on the grounds that these parents receive a ₹3,000 monthly old-age pension. This decision mandates authorities to process and release admissible claims within two months, setting aside rejection orders in 10 connected petitions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/haryana-government-employees-can-claim-medical-reimbursement.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This judgment holds considerable importance for government employees whose parents require hospitalisation or treatment but have limited pension income. The court's stance reinforces the principle that a modest social-security benefit does not automatically imply an elderly parent can independently cover their medical expenses. It clarifies that medical reimbursement cannot be denied based on technicalities that override statutory provisions.</p><p>The court specifically held that administrative instructions cannot supersede the Punjab Medical Attendance Rules, 1940. These statutory rules recognise wholly dependent family members, including parents, without imposing a rigid monetary ceiling for determining dependency. This distinction is crucial, as the dispute frequently arose when medical reimbursement claims were rejected because the assessed income of dependent family members exceeded a ceiling prescribed under government instructions.</p><p>In one illustrative case, a government employee sought reimbursement for the treatment of his 70-year-old mother, who suffered from chronic ailments and underwent inpatient treatment at an empanelled, government-approved hospital. The medical expenses for this treatment amounted to ₹3.73 lakh. However, the authorities rejected the claim, citing that the mother's assessed income surpassed the monthly ceiling of ₹3,500, a limit prescribed under government instructions issued in 2007. The court noted that neither the treatment, the medical bills, nor the expenditure itself was disputed; the claim was rejected purely on a technical interpretation of the income limit.</p><p>Further supporting its decision, the court considered a clarification issued on February 3, 2025, by Haryana's Director-General Health Services, which explicitly stated that the ₹3,000 old-age pension should not be treated as income for the purpose of medical reimbursement. The court observed that it is contradictory for the state to extend a social-security benefit to elderly citizens and then leverage the receipt of that very benefit as a justification to deny them access to medical reimbursement through their serving children.</p><p>The judgment also broadened the understanding of dependency, emphasising that it is not solely a question of income. Dependency can also encompass physical reliance, particularly when elderly parents suffer from serious or chronic illnesses and require continuous care, supervision, and support from their children. Additionally, the court addressed a woman's claim for medical reimbursement for her parents-in-law, ruling that denying such a benefit in those circumstances contravened Haryana's medical reimbursement policy.</p><p>For Haryana government employees, this ruling significantly strengthens their position, especially for those whose medical reimbursement claims for dependent parents have been rejected due to a small old-age pension or a strict interpretation of income limits. However, the court cautioned that this should not be misconstrued as an unconditional entitlement for every parent receiving a pension. The court's reasoning applies to genuine medical expenses and the careful assessment of dependency under the applicable rules. Therefore, eligibility and the exact amount payable will still be contingent on the relevant provisions and the specific facts of each claim.</p><p>The court has directed the competent authorities to promptly process, sanction, and release the admissible reimbursement amounts within two months of receiving a certified copy of the order, ensuring timely relief for affected employees.</p><p>What to watch: The timely implementation of the court's order and potential revisions to administrative guidelines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is comprehensive, well-structured, and incorporates all facts and nuances from both the summary and full text.</p>
