     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-18 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Rejects U.S. Lawmaker’s Criticism of Electoral Roll Revision</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Indian government has rejected remarks by U.S. Congresswoman Pramila Jayapal regarding the Election Commission’s revision of electoral rolls, labeling them &quot;unwarranted and uninformed.&quot; Jayapal had previously expressed concern over reports alleging the removal of 130 million voters from draft electoral rolls, suggesting the move mirrored efforts to undermine democratic voting rights.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/india-rejects-us-lawmakers-criticism-of-electoral-roll-revis.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Ministry of External Affairs spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal stated that the criticism amounts to interference in the electoral practices of a fellow democracy. The Election Commission is currently conducting an annual Summary Revision to enroll new voters and update records across several states and union territories, asserting that applicants do not require citizenship documentation for this process.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Succinctly captures the MEA's response to US lawmaker Pramila Jayapal's comments on the electoral roll revision.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/unwarranted-and-uninformed-india-rejects-us-lawmakers-comments-on-revision-of-electoral-rolls/article71593894.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
