     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-18 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Targets 100 Medals at Aichi-Nagoya Para Games</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Sports</span> <span>#Para Games</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Athletics</span></div>
       <p>India is targeting approximately 100 medals at the Aichi-Nagoya 2026 Asian Para Games, which officially opened in Japan on Sunday, October 18. The Indian contingent comprises 168 athletes, who will compete across 11 sports during the event, running until October 24. Para Athletics Head Coach Satyanarayana expressed strong confidence in the team's preparation and determination to deliver a robust performance.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/india-targets-100-medals-at-aichi-nagoya-para-games.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Satyanarayana highlighted that para athletics is expected to be a crucial component of India's medal campaign. He anticipates winning six medals in athletics on Monday, including two to three gold medals. Athlete Nishad Kumar is noted to be in good form, with expectations for him to achieve a personal best and potentially break a Games record. These early opportunities are seen as vital for building momentum.</p><p>The Indian team aims to build upon its historic performance at the previous edition in Hangzhou in 2023, where it secured a record 111 medals, including 29 gold, 31 silver, and 51 bronze, finishing fifth overall. The current contingent features a blend of experienced medal-winners and emerging talents, including prominent names such as javelin thrower Sumit Antil, para-badminton champion Pramod Bhagat, para-shooter Avani Lekhara, para-archer Sheetal Devi, and sprinter Simran Sharma.</p><p>The opening ceremony took place at the Paloma Mizuho Stadium in Nagoya, with para-badminton star Pramod Bhagat and para-athlete Simran Sharma carrying the Indian flag. Athletes are reportedly content with the arrangements, facilities, and support in Nagoya, fostering a positive and motivated environment. The focus now shifts to competition, with athletics events starting on Monday offering the first chance for India to add to its medal tally and reinforce its standing in Asian para sports.</p><p>What to watch: The start of athletics events on Monday for India's initial medal push.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report captures all pertinent details including the athlete count, the medal target, the coach's specific expectations, and the opening ceremony details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://sports.ndtv.com/othersports/india-targets-100-medal-mark-as-asian-para-games-begin-in-japan-12202182#publisher=newsstand" rel="noopener">NDTV Sports</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
