     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-18 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Maharashtra’s Kolhati Women Struggle to Preserve Tamasha Tradition</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Women from the Kolhati community in Maharashtra are working to preserve the state’s traditional Tamasha folk performance art. Despite their cultural contributions, these women frequently endure sexual exploitation, financial instability, and deep-seated social stigma. Their children often face systemic barriers, including a lack of access to formal education, which limits their ability to seek alternative livelihoods.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/maharashtras-kolhati-women-struggle-to-preserve-tamasha-trad.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Community members and activists are now attempting to address these challenges by working to break the intergenerational cycle of exploitation. These efforts focus on improving living conditions and educational opportunities for the next generation while simultaneously safeguarding the community's unique cultural heritage.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief captures the central tension between cultural preservation and social struggle described in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/maharashtra/maharashtras-tamasha-and-kolhati-communities-battle-exploitation-education-barriers-while-preserving-folk-traditions/article71588869.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
