     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-18 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>PM-VBRY Scheme Offers Up To ₹15,000 For First-Time Employees</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#EPFO</span> <span>#PM-VBRY</span> <span>#Employees</span> <span>#Incentive</span></div>
       <p>The Pradhan Mantri Viksit Bharat Rozgar Yojana (PM-VBRY) offers eligible first-time employees an incentive of up to ₹15,000, aiming to encourage formal employment by rewarding those entering the Employees’ Provident Fund (EPF) system for the first time. Administered through the Employees’ Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO), this scheme, as detailed by Livemint Money, provides a significant boost for individuals starting their careers in the formal sector. However, this benefit is not automatic and is subject to specific eligibility and service conditions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/pm-vbry-scheme-offers-up-to-15000-for-first-time-employees.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>To qualify for this incentive under Part A of PM-VBRY, an employee must be joining the formal workforce as a first-time EPF member. The scheme covers eligible individuals whose employment begins between August 1, 2025, and July 31, 2027. Crucially, employees must earn monthly wages of up to ₹1 lakh to be considered, alongside meeting other prescribed membership, wage, and employment conditions. It is essential for applicants to verify their EPF membership history and ensure their employer is diligently depositing contributions and filing the necessary Electronic Challan-cum-Returns (ECRs), as merely meeting the salary limit does not guarantee eligibility.</p><p>The incentive, which is equivalent to one month’s eligible EPF wage capped at ₹15,000, is disbursed in two instalments. The first payment becomes available after an employee completes six months of continuous service. To receive the second instalment, however, individuals must fulfil a further requirement: completing 12 months of service and participating in a prescribed financial literacy programme. It is important to note that this second instalment is not necessarily paid as immediately withdrawable cash; instead, scheme guidelines specify it is to be placed in an appropriate savings instrument or deposit account for a defined period.</p><p>For ordinary savers and taxpayers, understanding these conditions is paramount. The financial literacy course is a non-negotiable step for the second instalment, emphasizing the government's aim not just to provide an incentive but also to foster financial awareness. Payments under Part A are facilitated through Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) to Aadhaar-seeded bank accounts, utilizing the Aadhaar Bridge Payment System. Therefore, employees must ensure their bank and Aadhaar details are accurate and correctly linked, and that their employer is accurately reporting employment and EPF contributions. Practical steps for new employees include verifying first-time EPF membership, checking salary and joining-date criteria, maintaining continuous service, and completing the financial literacy programme. The official PM-VBRY portal is the recommended resource for detailed scheme information and guidance.</p><p>What to watch: Ensure all eligibility and service conditions are met for both instalments.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of all eligibility criteria, payment structure, and procedural requirements mentioned in the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/epfo-scheme-offers-up-to-15-000-to-first-time-employees-check-eligibility-salary-limit-and-payment-rules-11792317288159.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
