     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-18 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Scientists Discover Record-Speed Coral Bleaching Reversal Through Contact with Healthy Clones</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Coral Bleaching</span> <span>#Marine Biology</span> <span>#Climate Change</span></div>
       <p>Scientists have made a significant breakthrough, discovering a method to reverse coral bleaching at a record speed. For the first time, researchers observed severely bleached corals, previously on the brink of death, achieve full recovery through brief physical contact with healthy clones. This unprecedented recovery took place in a laboratory setting within just seven weeks, marking the fastest documented reversal to date, according to a study published in *Science Advances*.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/scientists-discover-record-speed-coral-bleaching-reversal-th.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This discovery offers new hope for coral ecosystems globally, which are increasingly threatened by marine heat waves driven by climate change. Experts estimate that corals could largely disappear within a few decades. Coral bleaching occurs when warming waters cause corals to expel beneficial algae, crucial for their growth. Their expulsion leaves corals pale, starving, and susceptible to harmful algae, with full recovery typically taking months to years.</p><p>The genesis of this finding traces back to 2025, when study co-author Steve Palumbi, a professor in Marine Sciences, observed an unusual phenomenon: two coral fragments from the same colony—one bleached and one healthy—accidentally made contact. Color returned to the bleached fragment at the point of contact. Lead author Danny Collins, an oceans Ph.D. student at Stanford Doerr School of Sustainability, then posed the critical question of reintroducing beneficial elements via contact, noting that &quot;While bleached corals may be dying, they are not dead yet.&quot;</p><p>To investigate, Collins designed an experiment with 10 pairs of coral fragments, each from the same animal and consisting of one bleached and one healthy piece. Five pairs touched, while the other five were kept a few millimeters apart. The bleached fragments in the touching pairs fully recovered within seven weeks, whereas those without contact died, demonstrating a clear link between physical contact and recovery.</p><p>The team explained that genetically identical corals recognize each other upon contact, leading to tissue fusion. Microscopic channels within the coral tissues align shortly after contact, creating a direct pathway for transport. Beneficial algae and nutrients flow from healthy fragments to their bleached counterparts, with initial movement observed within just three days of contact. Further tests showed that separating coral pairs after one week, or even five days, was sufficient for bleached fragments to receive enough algae to complete recovery independently in seven weeks. This indicated that the primary driver was rapid replication of algae *within* the bleached fragment, with algae doubling every four days during the second week of contact—ten times faster than in wild corals.</p><p>This groundbreaking research challenges the conventional view that bleached corals are a &quot;lost cause&quot; and opens new avenues for coral restoration. While various tools exist—like breeding heat-tolerant corals, cultivating nurseries, and transplanting microbiomes—this contact-based recovery presents a novel and potentially rapid approach. The observed success in a controlled laboratory environment provides a strong foundation for developing new strategies to combat coral degradation.</p><p>What to watch: Future research on applying this contact-based recovery method to larger reef systems and its efficacy in diverse natural ocean environments.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent reporting that synthesizes the full source text accurately, including scientific methodology and expert quotes.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scientists-reverse-coral.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
