     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-18 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Trump Waives Russian Oil Sanctions, India Gains Temporary Reprieve</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#World</span> <span>#US Politics</span> <span>#Sanctions</span> <span>#India</span></div>
       <p>U.S. President Donald Trump has temporarily waived key provisions of the Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act (Graham Act), pausing threatened punitive tariffs of up to 100% on countries, including India and China, that purchase Russian oil and gas. U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio explained that the administration is taking 60 days to study the broader economic and geopolitical impacts before deciding on its next steps. The Graham Act, passed by an overwhelming bipartisan majority in September, authorized additional 100% tariffs on goods from the five largest importers of Russian crude oil and natural gas if they made new purchases from October 18.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-18/trump-waives-russian-oil-sanctions-india-gains-temporary-rep.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>While framed as a 60-day review, the signed executive order specifies that the waivers will remain in effect until the President or Secretary of State determines further action is warranted, without an explicit automatic expiration. This decision aims to safeguard international energy markets amidst multiple compounding global crises, including volatility from the war involving Iran, Houthi attacks targeting Saudi Arabia, and the ongoing Russia-Ukraine war.</p><p>Secretary Rubio, scheduled to visit India on Monday, defended the temporary reprieve, acknowledging that while India has voluntarily tried to diversify its energy portfolio, a massive supplier pivot cannot happen &quot;from one day to the next.&quot; He emphasized India remains a crucial U.S. ally and partner. For India, this means a temporary shield from steep additional U.S. tariffs and trade restrictions, allowing refiners to continue sourcing discounted Russian oil without immediate penalties.</p><p>The decision has, however, ignited significant friction within Washington. Lawmakers from both political parties criticized the pause, arguing it undercuts Western efforts to choke off Moscow’s military funding. Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren accused Trump of disregarding Congress and enabling Russian aggression, calling on lawmakers to reverse the decision. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy also sharply criticized the move, stating that relaxing restrictions directly benefits Russia.</p><p>Trade analysts and the Global Trade Research Initiative caution that this is a temporary, reversible reprieve rather than a permanent policy shift. New Delhi still faces policy uncertainty once the 60-day review concludes. The move arrives ahead of high-level bilateral engagements, including Rubio’s visit to India, and could influence ongoing talks regarding an India-U.S. trade deal.</p><p>What to watch: The U.S. administration's decision after the 60-day review period.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive summary that covers the legislative context, the Rationale, the political friction, and the specific implications for India as detailed in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/explainers/60-days-no-punitive-tariffs-what-trumps-waiver-on-russian-oil-means-for-india-ws-klrt-10387458.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
